The warning message "Cannot find the 'CKEDITOR_VERSION' in the 'window' scope" and the CKEditor not loading issue likely arises because CKEditor is not correctly being attached to the window object or some CKEditor-related files are not being correctly referenced.

Let's tackle this step by step to ensure CKEditor is properly loaded and integrated into your Angular application.

### Step 1: Update CKEditorLoaderService

Ensure that the CKEditorLoaderService correctly loads CKEditor without relying on the CKEDITOR_VERSION property.

```typescript
// ckeditor-loader.service.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root',
})
export class CKEditorLoaderService {
  private isCKEditorLoaded = false;

  async loadCKEditor(): Promise<any> {
    if (this.isCKEditorLoaded) {
      return (window as any).ClassicEditor;
    }

    if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
      const { default: ClassicEditor } = await import('@ckeditor/ckeditor5-build-classic');
      (window as any).ClassicEditor = ClassicEditor;
      this.isCKEditorLoaded = true;
      return ClassicEditor;
    }
  }
}
```

### Step 2: Ensure Proper Imports and Usage in AddNewsComponent

Ensure your `AddNewsComponent` properly initializes CKEditor.

```typescript
// add-news.component.ts
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { FormBuilder, FormGroup, ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
import { NewsService } from '../news-events.service';
import { CKEditorLoaderService } from '../ckeditor-loader.service';
import { CKEditorModule } from '@ckeditor/ckeditor5-angular';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-add-news',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule, CKEditorModule],
  templateUrl: './add-news.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./add-news.component.scss']
})
export class AddNewsComponent implements OnInit {
  newsForm: FormGroup;
  public Editor: any;

  constructor(
    private fb: FormBuilder,
    private newsService: NewsService,
    private ckeditorLoader: CKEditorLoaderService
  ) {
    this.newsForm = this.fb.group({
      title: [''],
      content: [''],
      image: [null]
    });
  }

  async ngOnInit() {
    console.log('NewsEventsComponent init');
    this.Editor = await this.ckeditorLoader.loadCKEditor();
    console.log('CKEditor loaded:', this.Editor);
  }

  onFileSelected(event: any) {
    this.newsForm.patchValue({
      image: event.target.files[0]
    });
  }

  async onSubmit() {
    if (this.newsForm.valid) {
      const newsData = this.newsForm.value;
      const contentHtml = newsData.content; // This contains the HTML content from CKEditor

      let imageUrl = '';

      if (newsData.image) {
        imageUrl = await this.newsService.uploadImage(newsData.image);
      }

      this.newsService.addNews('instituteId', {
        title: newsData.title,
        content: contentHtml,
        imageUrl: imageUrl,
        date: new Date()
      }).then(() => {
        this.newsForm.reset();
      });
    }
  }
}
```

### Step 3: Ensure HTML Template is Correct

Ensure your HTML template for `AddNewsComponent` is correctly set up.

```html
<!-- add-news.component.html -->
<div class="add-news-container">
  <form [formGroup]="newsForm" (ngSubmit)="onSubmit()">
    <div>
      <label for="title">Title</label>
      <input id="title" formControlName="title" type="text" required />
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="content">Content</label>
      <ckeditor [editor]="Editor" formControlName="content"></ckeditor>
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="image">Image</label>
      <input id="image" type="file" (change)="onFileSelected($event)" />
    </div>
    <button type="submit">Add News</button>
  </form>
</div>
```

### Step 4: Check for Console Errors or Warnings

Check the browser console for any additional errors or warnings that might provide more information.

### Step 5: Import CKEditor CSS in Global Styles (Optional)

If needed, import the CKEditor CSS directly in your global styles file (`styles.scss` or `styles.css`).

```scss
/* styles.scss or styles.css */
@import '~@ckeditor/ckeditor5-build-classic/build/ckeditor.css';
```

### Step 6: Restart Angular Development Server

Restart the Angular development server to ensure all changes are applied.

```bash
ng serve
```

By following these steps, you should be able to resolve the issue with CKEditor not being visible in your UI. If the editor still does not appear, please provide any additional console errors or warnings for further troubleshooting.

