To set the color of the top bar (status bar) on mobile devices when your website is opened, you need to use the `theme-color` meta tag in the `<head>` section of your HTML document. This tag is supported by most modern mobile browsers and allows you to set the color of the browser's address bar and the status bar on the home screen when the site is added to the user's home screen.

Here's how you can implement it:

1. **Add the Meta Tag in HTML**:
   Add the following meta tag in the `<head>` section of your `index.html` file:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#YOUR_COLOR_CODE">
  <title>Your App</title>
  <!-- Other meta tags and links -->
</head>
<body>
  <app-root></app-root>
  <!-- Scripts -->
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `#YOUR_COLOR_CODE` with the desired color code for the top bar. For example, if you want the color to be blue, you would use `#0000FF`.

2. **Dynamically Change the Theme Color**:
   If you need to dynamically change the theme color based on some conditions in your Angular application, you can do this using JavaScript. Here’s an example of how you can achieve this in your `app.component.ts`:

```typescript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css']
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {

  constructor() { }

  ngOnInit() {
    this.setThemeColor('#YOUR_DYNAMIC_COLOR_CODE'); // Replace with your dynamic color logic
  }

  setThemeColor(color: string) {
    const metaThemeColor = document.querySelector("meta[name=theme-color]");
    if (metaThemeColor) {
      metaThemeColor.setAttribute("content", color);
    } else {
      const meta = document.createElement('meta');
      meta.name = 'theme-color';
      meta.content = color;
      document.getElementsByTagName('head')[0].appendChild(meta);
    }
  }
}
```

Replace `#YOUR_DYNAMIC_COLOR_CODE` with the color code you want to set dynamically. You can call the `setThemeColor` method with different color values based on your application's state or route.

### Example: Changing Theme Color Based on Route

If you want to change the theme color based on the active route, you can use Angular's `Router` service to listen for route changes and update the theme color accordingly:

```typescript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { Router, NavigationEnd } from '@angular/router';
import { filter } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css']
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {

  constructor(private router: Router) { }

  ngOnInit() {
    this.router.events.pipe(
      filter(event => event instanceof NavigationEnd)
    ).subscribe((event: NavigationEnd) => {
      if (event.url === '/home') {
        this.setThemeColor('#FFFFFF'); // White color for home route
      } else if (event.url === '/dashboard') {
        this.setThemeColor('#0000FF'); // Blue color for dashboard route
      }
    });
  }

  setThemeColor(color: string) {
    const metaThemeColor = document.querySelector("meta[name=theme-color]");
    if (metaThemeColor) {
      metaThemeColor.setAttribute("content", color);
    } else {
      const meta = document.createElement('meta');
      meta.name = 'theme-color';
      meta.content = color;
      document.getElementsByTagName('head')[0].appendChild(meta);
    }
  }
}
```

This example changes the theme color to white when the user navigates to the home route and blue when the user navigates to the dashboard route. You can customize the color values and routes based on your application's requirements.

