To integrate CKEditor in your Angular application using standalone components without an app.module, you can indeed use `app.config.ts` to handle necessary imports. Follow these steps to correctly configure CKEditor:

### 1. Install Required Packages
Make sure you have the necessary CKEditor packages installed:

```bash
npm install @ckeditor/ckeditor5-angular @ckeditor/ckeditor5-build-classic
```

### 2. Create a Typings File for CKEditor
Create a `typings.d.ts` file if it doesn't exist:

**src/typings.d.ts:**
```typescript
declare module '@ckeditor/ckeditor5-build-classic' {
  const ClassicEditorBuild: any;
  export = ClassicEditorBuild;
}
```

### 3. Create CKEditor Component
Create a standalone CKEditor component:

**src/app/ckeditor/ckeditor.component.ts:**
```typescript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { CKEditorModule } from '@ckeditor/ckeditor5-angular';
import * as ClassicEditor from '@ckeditor/ckeditor5-build-classic';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-ckeditor',
  standalone: true,
  templateUrl: './ckeditor.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./ckeditor.component.css'],
  imports: [CKEditorModule]
})
export class CKEditorComponent implements OnInit {
  public Editor = ClassicEditor;
  public editorData = '<p>Hello from CKEditor 5!</p>';

  ngOnInit() {}

  onReady(editor: any) {
    console.log('Editor is ready', editor);
  }

  onChange({ editor }: any) {
    const data = editor.getData();
    console.log(data);
  }
}
```

**src/app/ckeditor/ckeditor.component.html:**
```html
<ckeditor
  [editor]="Editor"
  [(ngModel)]="editorData"
  (ready)="onReady($event)"
  (change)="onChange($event)"
></ckeditor>
```

### 4. Main Application Component
Update your main application component to include the CKEditor component:

**src/app/app.component.ts:**
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { CKEditorComponent } from './ckeditor/ckeditor.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
