To ensure that the content displayed in your news articles is stripped of any font styles and displayed as normal text, you can sanitize the HTML content before displaying it. Angular provides built-in sanitization services that you can use to achieve this.

### Step-by-Step Solution

1. **Sanitize HTML Content**:

   Use Angular's `DomSanitizer` service to sanitize the HTML content and remove any unwanted styles.

2. **Display Sanitized Content**:

   Bind the sanitized content to the template for display.

### Implementation

1. **Import DomSanitizer**:

   In your component, import `DomSanitizer` and inject it in the constructor.

   ```typescript
   import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
   import { DomSanitizer, SafeHtml } from '@angular/platform-browser';
   import { FormBuilder, FormGroup } from '@angular/forms';
   import { NewsService } from '../services/news.service';
   import { CustomizationService } from '../services/customization.service';

   @Component({
     selector: 'app-add-news',
     templateUrl: './add-news.component.html',
     styleUrls: ['./add-news.component.css']
   })
   export class AddNewsComponent implements OnInit {
     newsForm: FormGroup;
     sanitizedContent: SafeHtml;

     constructor(
       private fb: FormBuilder,
       private newsService: NewsService,
       private customizationService: CustomizationService,
       private sanitizer: DomSanitizer
     ) {}

     ngOnInit(): void {
       this.newsForm = this.fb.group({
         title: [''],
         content: [''],
         image: [null]
       });
     }

     onSubmit(): void {
       if (this.newsForm.valid) {
         const newsData = this.newsForm.value;
         const contentHtml = this.sanitizeContent(newsData.content);

         // Further processing and submission
       }
     }

     sanitizeContent(content: string): SafeHtml {
       // Remove font styles using a regex or DOM manipulation if needed
       const strippedContent = content.replace(/<style[\s\S]*?<\/style>/gi, '');
       return this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml(strippedContent);
     }
   }
   ```

2. **Display Sanitized Content**:

   In your template, bind the sanitized content for display.

   ```html
   <div class="news-container">
     <h1>Our News & Events articles</h1>
     <div class="newslist">
       <mat-card *ngFor="let news of newsList" matRipple (click)="viewArticle(news.title, news.id)">
         <mat-card-header>
           <mat-card-title>{{ news.title }}</mat-card-title>
           <mat-card-subtitle>{{ news.date.toDate() | date }}</mat-card-subtitle>
         </mat-card-header>
         <img mat-card-image src="../../assets/newsbg.jpg" *ngIf="!news.imageUrl" class="news-image">
         <img mat-card-image [src]="news.imageUrl" *ngIf="news.imageUrl" class="news-image">
         <mat-divider></mat-divider>
         <mat-card-content>
           <div [innerHTML]="sanitizeContent(news.content)" class="news-content"></div>
           <p class="readmore">Read article -></p>
         </mat-card-content>
       </mat-card>
     </div>
   </div>
   ```

### Explanation

1. **Sanitizing Content**: The `sanitizeContent` method removes any unwanted font styles from the content using a regex that strips out `<style>` tags. The `DomSanitizer` service then sanitizes the HTML content to prevent XSS attacks.

2. **Displaying Content**: The sanitized content is bound to the `innerHTML` property of a `div` in the template, ensuring that the content is displayed without any custom font styles.

You can adjust the sanitization process to remove other unwanted elements or attributes as needed. This approach ensures that the content is displayed in a clean, consistent manner across your application.

