To make your Node.js application respond to requests in the format `anyvalue.growthtutorials.in:8080` instead of `growthtutorials.in:8080/validate-subdomain/anyvalue`, you need to configure your server to parse the subdomain from the request's hostname directly, and then validate it accordingly. This typically involves:

1. Configuring your DNS or server settings to handle wildcard subdomains.
2. Adjusting your Node.js application to extract the subdomain from the request and validate it.

### Step 1: Wildcard Subdomain Configuration

First, ensure your DNS settings include a wildcard entry (`*.growthtutorials.in`). This allows any subdomain of `growthtutorials.in` to point to your server where your Node.js application is hosted.

If you're using NGINX as a reverse proxy in front of your Node.js app, ensure NGINX is configured to pass the full host to your application. Here's a snippet you might use in your NGINX configuration:

```nginx
server {
    listen 8080;
    server_name *.growthtutorials.in;

    location / {
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000; # Assuming your Node.js app runs on port 3000
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Node.js Application Changes

Modify your Node.js application to extract the subdomain from the host header and process it accordingly. Here’s how you can adjust your `server.js`:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const { initializeApp } = require('firebase-admin/app');
const { getFirestore } = require('firebase-admin/firestore');

const app = express();
const port = 3000;

// Firebase configuration as previously set up
initializeApp({
    // Your Firebase initialization here
});

const firestore = getFirestore();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
    const host = req.headers.host;
    const subdomain = host.split('.')[0];  // Assumes the subdomain is the first part of the host
    req.subdomain = subdomain;
    next();
});

app.get('/', async (req, res) => {
    const subdomain = req.subdomain; // Extracted subdomain from the request
    try {
        const subdomainsSnapshot = await firestore.collection('registrations')
            .where('subdomain', '==', subdomain)
            .get();

        if (!subdomainsSnapshot.empty) {
            res.status(200).send('Subdomain is registered');
        } else {
            res.status(404).send('Subdomain not registered');
        }
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error querying Firestore:', error);
        res.status(500).send('Server error');
    }
});

app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
```

### Testing and Verification

After making these changes:
- Restart your Node.js application.
- Make sure NGINX (or your respective web server) is configured and restarted if needed.
- Test by accessing `http://subdomain.growthtutorials.in:8080` in your browser or using a tool like `curl`.

These modifications will allow your Node.js application to directly respond to subdomain-based URLs and validate them accordingly.

