To change the body background color based on an element reaching the top left corner of the viewport on scroll, you can use Angular to detect the scroll event and apply the background color dynamically. This cannot be done purely with SCSS, as SCSS does not handle dynamic behavior.

Here’s how you can achieve this using Angular:

### Step-by-Step Solution

1. **Set Up Angular Project**: Make sure you have an Angular project set up.

2. **Create the Component**: If you haven't already, create a component for your sections.

3. **Add Scroll Event Listener**: In the component, add a scroll event listener to detect when the element reaches the top left corner of the viewport.

4. **Change Background Color**: Update the background color of the body based on the scroll position.

### Example Implementation

**app.component.html**:
```html
<div class="section" id="section1">Section 1</div>
<div class="section" id="section2">Section 2</div>
<div class="section" id="section3">Section 3</div>
```

**app.component.css**:
```css
.section {
  height: 100vh;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #000;
}

/* Add transition to body for smooth background color change */
body {
  transition: background-color 0.5s;
}
```

**app.component.ts**:
```typescript
import { Component, HostListener, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css']
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {

  ngOnInit() {
    this.onWindowScroll(); // Initial check
  }

  @HostListener('window:scroll', [])
  onWindowScroll() {
    const section1 = document.getElementById('section1');
    const section2 = document.getElementById('section2');
    const section3 = document.getElementById('section3');

    const section1Top = section1.getBoundingClientRect().top;
    const section2Top = section2.getBoundingClientRect().top;
    const section3Top = section3.getBoundingClientRect().top;

    if (section1Top <= 0 && section1Top + section1.offsetHeight > 0) {
      document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'lightblue';
    } else if (section2Top <= 0 && section2Top + section2.offsetHeight > 0) {
      document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'lightgreen';
    } else if (section3Top <= 0 && section3Top + section3.offsetHeight > 0) {
      document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'lightcoral';
    } else {
      document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'white'; // Default color
    }
  }
}
```

### Explanation

1. **HTML Structure**: Create sections in your HTML file. Each section has a unique ID for easy reference.
2. **CSS Styling**: Define the height of each section and add some basic styling. Add a transition to the body to smooth the background color change.
3. **TypeScript Code**: 
   - Use `@HostListener('window:scroll', [])` to listen for scroll events.
   - In the `onWindowScroll` method, get the top position of each section relative to the viewport using `getBoundingClientRect().top`.
   - Check if a section is at the top of the viewport and update the body's background color accordingly.

### Additional Notes

- **Optimization**: For better performance, consider debouncing the scroll event listener.
- **Responsiveness**: Ensure the sections are designed to handle different screen sizes and resolutions.

This approach will dynamically change the body's background color as different sections reach the top of the viewport during scrolling.

