Yes, you can achieve the sticky header effect using only CSS (SCSS). Here's how you can do it with CSS to handle the shrinking header and making it sticky without the need for JavaScript:

### 1. HTML Structure

Update your HTML structure to include the necessary elements:

```html
<div class="container">
    <header class="float-header">
        <h1 class="institute-name">{{ subdomain }}</h1>
    </header>
    <main id="main-content">
        <mat-divider></mat-divider>
        <app-news-events></app-news-events>
    </main>
</div>
```

### 2. SCSS Styling

Use CSS transitions and the `position: sticky` property to handle the sticky behavior and transitions.

```scss
$primary-color: #333; // replace with your theme's primary dark color
$transition-duration: 0.3s;

.container {
    min-height: 100vh;
}

.float-header {
    background-color: $primary-color;
    height: 70vh;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 1000;
    transition: height $transition-duration, background-color $transition-duration;

    .institute-name {
        font-size: 90px;
        color: white;
        transition: font-size $transition-duration;
    }
}

#main-content {
    padding-top: 70vh;
}

// Change header style when scrolled
.float-header.is-scrolled {
    height: 60px;
    .institute-name {
        font-size: 32px;
    }
}
```

### 3. Adding Scroll Effects with CSS

Use CSS to change the styles when the page is scrolled. Although CSS alone cannot detect scroll events, we can use the `:sticky` pseudo-class to change the styles based on scroll position.

Unfortunately, CSS alone does not provide a direct way to change styles based on scroll position without using JavaScript. However, we can still use a combination of CSS and a small amount of JavaScript to add or remove classes based on scroll position.

### Combining CSS and JavaScript for Scroll Effects

To fully achieve the desired effect, we will need to use a small amount of JavaScript to add a class to the header when the page is scrolled.

#### Updated `home.component.ts`

```typescript
import { Component, OnInit, HostListener } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-home',
  templateUrl: './home.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./home.component.scss']
})
export class HomeComponent implements OnInit {
  subdomain: string = 'Your Institute Name'; // Replace with actual logic to fetch institute name

  constructor() { }

  ngOnInit(): void { }

  @HostListener('window:scroll', [])
  onWindowScroll() {
    const header = document.querySelector('.float-header');
    if (window.pageYOffset > 60) {
      header?.classList.add('is-scrolled');
    } else {
      header?.classList.remove('is-scrolled');
    }
  }
}
```

This small script adds or removes the `is-scrolled` class to the `.float-header` element based on the scroll position.

### Summary

- **HTML**: Keep the structure clean.
- **SCSS**: Use `position: sticky` and transitions for smooth effects.
- **JavaScript**: Minimal script to toggle the class based on scroll position.

This approach ensures a smooth and responsive header transition effect, combining the power of CSS and minimal JavaScript for enhanced functionality. If you encounter any issues or need further adjustments, feel free to let me know!

