The error message indicates that Certbot could not automatically find a matching server block for your domain in the Nginx configuration. This is because you need to have a server block defined with the `server_name` directive set to your domain name before running Certbot. 

### Step-by-Step Guide to Fix the Issue:

1. **Create a Server Block for Your Domain:**

   Edit the Nginx configuration file to include a server block for your domain. This server block will temporarily handle HTTP traffic and allow Certbot to generate the SSL certificate.

   ```bash
   sudo nano /etc/nginx/conf.d/growthtutorials.conf
   ```

   Add the following configuration to the file:

   ```nginx
   server {
       listen 80;
       server_name growthtutorials.in www.growthtutorials.in;

       root /usr/share/nginx/html;  # You can change this to your actual document root later
       index index.html index.htm;

       location / {
           try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
       }
   }
   ```

2. **Test Nginx Configuration:**

   Test the configuration to ensure there are no syntax errors:

   ```bash
   sudo nginx -t
   ```

3. **Restart Nginx:**

   Restart Nginx to apply the new configuration:

   ```bash
   sudo systemctl restart nginx
   ```

4. **Run Certbot to Obtain SSL Certificate:**

   Now that the server block is configured, run Certbot to obtain and install the SSL certificate:

   ```bash
   sudo certbot --nginx -d growthtutorials.in -d www.growthtutorials.in
   ```

   Follow the prompts to complete the setup. Certbot will modify the Nginx configuration to include the SSL settings.

5. **Update the Document Root:**

   After obtaining the SSL certificate, update the Nginx configuration to point to your actual document root where your static website files will be placed:

   ```bash
   sudo nano /etc/nginx/conf.d/growthtutorials.conf
   ```

   Update the `root` directive to the actual path of your static website files:

   ```nginx
   server {
       listen 80;
       server_name growthtutorials.in www.growthtutorials.in;

       root /path/to/your/html/folder;
       index index.html index.htm;

       location / {
           try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
       }
   }

   server {
       listen 443 ssl;
       server_name growthtutorials.in www.growthtutorials.in;

       root /path/to/your/html/folder;
       index index.html index.htm;

       ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/growthtutorials.in/fullchain.pem;
       ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/growthtutorials.in/privkey.pem;
       include /etc/letsencrypt/options-ssl-nginx.conf;
       ssl_dhparam /etc/letsencrypt/ssl-dhparams.pem;

       location / {
           try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
       }
   }
   ```

6. **Test and Restart Nginx:**

   Test the configuration again:

   ```bash
   sudo nginx -t
   ```

   Restart Nginx to apply the changes:

   ```bash
   sudo systemctl restart nginx
   ```

By following these steps, you will configure Nginx to serve your static website over HTTPS using a free SSL certificate from Let's Encrypt. If you encounter any issues or need further assistance, feel free to ask!

